.TH DOS2UNIX 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
dos2unix, unix2dos \- convert text between DOS and Unix line endings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dos2unix
.RB [ \-u | \-d ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B unix2dos
.RB [ \-u | \-d ]
.RI [ file ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dos2unix
converts DOS line endings, a carriage return (CR) followed by a line
feed (LF), to Unix line endings, a lone LF.
.B unix2dos
converts the other way.
Each
.I file
is converted in place.
With no
.I file
operands, the command reads standard input and writes standard output.
.PP
The conversion works on bytes.
.B dos2unix
deletes every CR, and
.B unix2dos
deletes every CR and writes a CR before every LF.
So a CR that is not followed by LF is deleted in both directions,
.B unix2dos
leaves CR LF pairs unchanged, and converting a file twice has no further
effect.
All other bytes are copied unchanged.
.PP
Each
.I file
is written to a temporary file next to it, named
.I file
with six characters appended, which then replaces
.IR file .
If
.I file
is a symbolic link, the file it refers to is converted in this way and
the link is kept.
Files are converted in order, and the first error stops the command,
leaving the remaining files unconverted.
A
.I file
named
.B \-
is a file, not standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
The command's name selects the default direction, and an option
overrides it.
.B \-u
and
.B \-d
cannot be combined: giving both is a usage error.
Options may follow
.I file
operands, unless
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
is set.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.TP
.B \-u
Convert to Unix line endings, as
.B dos2unix
does by default.
.TP
.B \-d
Convert to DOS line endings, as
.B unix2dos
does by default.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-d
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
When it has the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
each converted
.I file
is read-only.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted to the console's code page.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Invalid usage, or a
.I file
could not be opened, written, or replaced.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert C sources to Unix line endings in place:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ dos2unix *.c *.h
.EE
.RE
.PP
Do the same for a whole source tree:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src \-name \(aq*.[ch]\(aq \-exec dos2unix {} +
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write a copy of a file with DOS line endings, leaving the original
unchanged:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ unix2dos <notes.txt >notes\-dos.txt
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from the standalone
.B dos2unix
and
.B unix2dos
commands common on Linux:
.IP \(bu 2
Binary files are not detected and skipped, so they are corrupted.
.IP \(bu 2
Every CR is deleted, not only those before LF, so text with old Mac line
endings loses its line breaks.
.IP \(bu 2
Text in UTF-8 or another ASCII-compatible encoding converts correctly,
and a byte order mark is copied unchanged, but UTF-16 text is corrupted.
.IP \(bu 2
Besides
.BR \-\-help ,
the only options are
.B \-u
and
.BR \-d .
There is no option to write a new file or to keep the date.
.PP
Standard input and output are in binary mode, so line endings are
exactly as described above.
Without
.I file
operands, an error writing standard output is not reported, and the
exit status is 0 unless the output was a closed pipe.
.PP
A converted
.I file
is always a new file, even when nothing changed.
It gets the current modification time, attributes such as read-only and
hidden are not kept, and other hard links to the original keep the old
contents.
A read-only
.I file
is converted anyway.
If
.I file
cannot be replaced, for example because another program has it open,
the command fails and leaves the temporary file behind.
A read error partway through a
.I file
is taken as its end, so the part read before it replaces
.I file
and no error is reported.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR tr (1),
.BR xxd (1)
